<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Backtest note: does the engine's valuation signal predict returns?</title>
<style>{{ css }}</style></head>
<body>
<div class="masthead">
  <div class="house">SaaS Valuation Engine · Quantitative Research<small>Backtest note</small></div>
  <div class="meta"><b>18 US software companies</b><br>{{ today }}</div>
</div>

<div class="kicker">Point-in-time backtest · {{ first }} to {{ last }}</div>
<h1>Cheap for its growth has paid, but the evidence is three years deep</h1>
<p class="summary">Ranking the {{ n_companies }} companies each quarter by the engine's valuation score, using only filings and
prices public on the day, gave a mean 12-month rank information coefficient (IC) of <b>{{ "%.2f"|format(eng.mean_ic) }}</b>
(Newey-West t {{ "%.1f"|format(eng.ic_t_nw) }}), positive on {{ "%.0f"|format(eng.ic_hit_rate * 100) }}% of
{{ n_dates }} start dates. The cheapest third beat the most expensive third by {{ "%.0f"|format(eng.mean_tercile_spread * 100) }}
points over the following year on average. The result survives the checks below, but rests on only three
non-overlapping years in one market regime, and should be read as encouraging rather than proven.</p>

<h2>Question and method</h2>
<p><b>Question.</b> On past dates, did the companies the engine called cheap go on to beat the ones it called expensive?</p>
<p><b>Point in time.</b> For each quarter-end from {{ first }}, every SEC fact filed after that day is removed and the
engine's own extraction rebuilds the financials as they stood then, including restatements. Prices are that day's
closes, betas are regressed on SPY over the prior two years, and the risk-free rate is that day's 10-year Treasury
yield. The engine's comps, DCF and ranking then run unchanged. Forward returns are dividend-adjusted.</p>
<p><b>Measures.</b> The rank IC is the Spearman correlation between a signal and the next 12 months' returns across
companies on one date. The tercile spread is the average return of the top third minus the bottom third. Start dates are
quarterly but returns run 12 months, so windows overlap: t-statistics use Newey-West standard errors.</p>

<figure>{{ svg.signals }}<figcaption>Grey: naive baselines. Source: this backtest; 12-month horizon, {{ first12 }} to {{ last12 }} start dates.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="two">
  <figure>{{ svg.ic }}<figcaption>Orange: mean across start dates.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure>{{ svg.spread }}<figcaption>Red bars: the expensive third did better.</figcaption></figure>
</div>

<h2>Results</h2>
<table class="data">
  <tr><th>Signal</th><th>Mean IC 12m</th><th>t (NW)</th><th>IC &gt; 0</th><th>Tercile spread 12m</th><th>Mean IC 6m</th></tr>
  {% for label in sum12.index %}
  <tr><td>{{ label }}</td><td>{{ "%+.2f"|format(sum12.at[label, "mean_ic"]) }}</td><td>{{ "%.1f"|format(sum12.at[label, "ic_t_nw"]) }}</td>
    <td>{{ "%.0f"|format(sum12.at[label, "ic_hit_rate"] * 100) }}%</td><td>{{ "%+.0f"|format(sum12.at[label, "mean_tercile_spread"] * 100) }} pts</td>
    <td>{{ "%+.2f"|format(sum6.at[label, "mean_ic"]) if label in sum6.index else "n/a" }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>
<p class="note">The signal is weaker at six months ({{ "%+.2f"|format(sum6.at[signals.score, "mean_ic"]) }}), consistent with valuation
gaps closing slowly. Plain cheapness (low EV/revenue) is {{ "%.0f"|format(corr_cheap * 100) }}% rank-correlated with the engine
score yet earns an IC of only {{ "%.2f"|format(cheap.mean_ic) }}: the value is in adjusting price for growth, not in low
multiples as such. Momentum carries no information here.</p>

<h2>Robustness</h2>
<ul class="watch">
  <li><b>Independent years.</b> Using one start date per year removes the overlap: ICs of
    {% for d, v in annual.items() %}{{ "%+.2f"|format(v) }} ({{ d[:4] }}){{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}.
    One of three years was negative.</li>
  <li><b>No single company.</b> Dropping each company in turn leaves the mean IC between {{ "%.2f"|format(loo_min) }}
    (without {{ loo_low }}) and {{ "%.2f"|format(loo_max) }}.</li>
  <li><b>Chance.</b> Shuffling the signal across companies on each date 5,000 times, {{ "%.1f"|format(perm_p * 100) }}% of
    shuffles matched the observed mean IC (95th percentile of the null: {{ "%.2f"|format(null95) }}). This null still treats
    overlapping windows as more independent than they are, so the true p-value is higher.</li>
  <li><b>Design chosen after the fact.</b> The comps regression on revenue growth was selected in 2026 for its fit. Rerun with
    the rejected alternative ({{ alt_reg }}), the score's IC is {{ "%.2f"|format(alt.mean_ic) }} (t {{ "%.1f"|format(alt.ic_t_nw) }}),
    so the result does not depend on that choice.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Limitations</h2>
<ul class="watch">
  <li><b>Survivorship.</b> The 18 companies were chosen in 2026 as today's established SaaS names. Companies that failed,
    were acquired or shrank out of relevance in 2023-25 are missing, which can flatter any signal.</li>
  <li><b>Small, correlated sample.</b> {{ n_companies }} companies in one sector move together, so the cross-section holds far
    less independent information than its size suggests, and terciles are six stocks each.</li>
  <li><b>One regime.</b> 2023-26 combined rising-then-stable rates and an AI-driven rotation within software. A signal that
    rewards cheap-for-growth may behave differently in another cycle.</li>
  <li><b>Other design choices.</b> DCF parameters (terminal growth, mature margin, fade length) were set with today's knowledge.
    Only the comps choice was tested for sensitivity here.</li>
  <li><b>No costs or capacity.</b> Returns are gross, equal-weighted and assume trading at the close on each date.</li>
</ul>
<div class="disclosures"><p>Educational project, not investment advice. Data: SEC EDGAR companyfacts (all filed versions,
filtered by filing date) and Yahoo Finance prices. Reproduce with <code>python -m saas_valuation.research.backtest</code>.</p></div>
</body>
</html>
